--TEST--
`Core\Test`'s three equality members compare subject-first, label both sides by name and differ only at the object row -- `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 4's table is one comparison with two substitutions at the object row, so
// this case asks each member the same questions and reads where they part.

class Cell implements Comparable {
    public function constructor(public int $n) {}
    public function compareTo(self $other): int { return $this->n <=> $other->n; }
}

class Plain {
    public function constructor(public int $n) {}
}

class Box {
    public function constructor(public string $label, public array<int> $rows) {}
}

// `rule:errors/record-transformations`'s redaction row, asked of a failure message rather than of a
// dump: whether two secrets differ is reportable, what they hold is not.
class Vault {
    public function constructor(public secret string $token, public int $id) {}
}

int $refused = 0;

// The rows every member shares -- `rule:expressions/equality-semantics`'s scalar and `array<T>` table,
// read once rather than three times.
int $one = 1;
int $two = 2;
Core\Test::assertSame($one, 1);
Core\Test::assertEquals($one, 1);
echo "a scalar agrees under both\n";

array<int> $xs = [1, 2, 3];
array<int> $ys = [1, 2, 3];
array<int> $zs = [1, 9, 3];
Core\Test::assertSame($xs, $ys);
Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep($xs, $ys);
echo "an array agrees under both\n";

// The object row, where the three part company: identity, `Comparable`, and
// the structural walk.
Cell $left = new Cell(4);
Cell $right = new Cell(4);
Core\Test::assertEquals($left, $right);
Core\Test::assertSame($left, $left);
echo "a comparable object is equal but not the same\n";

try { Core\Test::assertSame($left, $right); } catch (Throwable $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $e->message, "\n"; }

// Both sides are named rather than numbered, so a call written in PHPUnit's
// order still reads correctly.
try { Core\Test::assertSame($one, $two); } catch (Throwable $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $e->message, "\n"; }

// `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2's trailing bag -- § 4's own `{message: ...}`, in front of the
// report rather than replacing it.
try { Core\Test::assertEquals($one, $two, {message: "a fresh counter is one"}); } catch (Throwable $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $e->message, "\n"; }

// The deep walk reports *where*, in the spelling a program subscripts with.
try { Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep($xs, $zs); } catch (Throwable $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $e->message, "\n"; }

// A non-`Comparable` object names the member that walks it. `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`
// refuses this where it is written; until `nvs_types` makes that refusal it is
// this throw -- `nvs_stdlib::test`'s own known gap 1.
Plain $p = new Plain(1);
Plain $q = new Plain(1);
try { Core\Test::assertEquals($p, $q); } catch (Throwable $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $e->message, "\n"; }
Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep($p, $q);
echo "the same object walks equal where `Comparable` refuses to answer\n";

// One level in, and the path names the property it descended through.
Box $b1 = new Box("a", $xs);
Box $b2 = new Box("a", $zs);
try { Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep($b1, $b2); } catch (Throwable $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $e->message, "\n"; }

Vault $v1 = new Vault("alpha", 7);
Vault $v2 = new Vault("beta", 7);
try { Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep($v1, $v2); } catch (Throwable $e) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo $e->message, "\n"; }

// Counted rather than read off the lines above: a member that stops refusing
// fails here even where its own line still looks plausible.
echo "refused=", $refused, "\n";
--EXPECT--
a scalar agrees under both
an array agrees under both
a comparable object is equal but not the same
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is a `Cell`, `$expected` is a `Cell`
Core\Test::assertSame failed: `$actual` is 1, `$expected` is 2
a fresh counter is one: Core\Test::assertEquals failed: `$actual` is 1, `$expected` is 2
Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep failed: the two differ at `$actual["1"]`: `$actual` is 2, `$expected` is 9
Core\Test::assertEquals compares an object only through `Comparable`, and a `Plain` declares no `compareTo` — write `Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep` for a structural comparison, or `Core\Test::assertSame` for identity
the same object walks equal where `Comparable` refuses to answer
Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep failed: the two differ at `$actual->rows["1"]`: `$actual` is 2, `$expected` is 9
Core\Test::assertEqualsDeep failed: the two differ at `$actual->token`: `$actual` is «redacted», `$expected` is «redacted»
refused=7
